1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,543
Chapter nineteen — rate cards.

2
00:00:03,603 --> 00:00:11,049
Every number so far rests on what
an hour of someone’s time costs.

3
00:00:11,109 --> 00:00:18,457
That is a rate card, and it is editable.

4
00:00:18,517 --> 00:00:32,192
A loaded rate per role, and per skill —
set them once and every estimate moves.

5
00:00:32,252 --> 00:00:44,491
By skill, or by role type — whichever
way your finance team thinks.

6
00:00:44,551 --> 00:00:52,419
And there is a GSA baseline.

7
00:00:52,479 --> 00:01:02,424
Turn it on and anything priced above
the schedule ceiling is flagged.

8
00:01:02,484 --> 00:01:15,233
Useful when the rate you are modelling
has to survive a contracting officer.

9
00:01:15,293 --> 00:01:21,303
People are only part of the bill.

10
00:01:21,363 --> 00:01:33,230
Twenty-two documents — each one written
and maintained by someone, and priced.

11
00:01:33,290 --> 00:01:43,412
Three tools, a hundred and thirty-five
dollars a month in licences.

12
00:01:43,472 --> 00:01:53,916
Against sixty-seven thousand a month of
effort removed — two point three FTE freed.

13
00:01:53,976 --> 00:02:02,920
Net impact, and the payback, computed
the same way the cloud bill is.

14
00:02:02,980 --> 00:02:08,583
Automation you can defend
with a number, not a slogan.
